    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#ConsumedPower -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#ConsumedPower">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#Power"/>
        <owl:disjointWith rdf:resource="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#GeneratedPower"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Formal Definition (DL)
power:Power ⊑BFO:Quality
power:GeneratedPower ⊑ power:Power
power:ConsumedPower⊑power:Power</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO - power:GeneratedPower [001 - 002]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#ConsumerRole -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#ConsumerRole">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000023"/>
        <rdfs:comment>BFO Classification
BFO Type: BFO:Role
The class power:ConsumerRole represents a specialization, characterizing the role assumed by an instance of power:FPSO when it acts as an energy consumer in the system, being a context-dependent entity that describes the participation of the FPSO as an energy receiver in a given operational scenario, being able to coexist with other energy roles simultaneously and not being a fixed or inherent characteristic of the material entity that instantiates it.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO - power:ConsumerRole [009-001]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#GeneratedPower -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#GeneratedPower">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#Power"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Formal Definition (DL)
power:Power ⊑BFO:Quality
power:GeneratedPower ⊑ power:Power
power:ConsumedPower⊑power:Power</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO - power:ConsumedPower  [001 - 003]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#LoadSharingStrategy -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#LoadSharingStrategy">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/core/Core/InformationContentEntity"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Suggested Reuse
No direct external import is required. This should remain local because it is application-control semantics.

Rationale
The necessary and exclusive instances equal, proportional, and merit_order are not material entities or processes by themselves. They are strategy specifications used by the BUS to govern balancing behavior.

Formal Definition (DL)
power:LoadSharingStrategy⊑BFO:GenericallyDependentContinuant

Recommended individuals
power:equal
power:proportional
power:merit_order

Elucidation
A LoadSharingStrategy is a specification that determines how the BUS distributes demand across connected generation sources.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO - power:LoadSharingStrategy [005-001]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#Power -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#Power">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000019"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Suggested Reuse
Conceptually compatible with the SEAS notion that electric power systems can produce or consume electric power.

Rationale
Power here is not the physical equipment, the process, or the information artifact. It is the electrical power quality/quantity borne by systems or involved in generation/consumption contexts. Power is not a Material Entity, and it is not a GDC in the usual physical sense. The electrical power output of a generator or the power demand of a bus. It implies: the best BFO reading is: a power instance is a quality, because in BFO, a quality is a specifically dependent continuant, something that depends on a bearer and does not need a separate realization process, the way a role or disposition does.

So I would separate the options like this:
Material Entity? No. Material entities are bearers such as turbines, cables, buses, generators, and platforms. Power is not the equipment; it is something of the equipment or system.
GDC? Normally no. A GDC could be something like a formula, a demand profile document, a measurement record, a specification sheet, or a dataset entry. If you store “Power = 4.5 MW” in a database, that record is a GDC / information artifact; the underlying physical power itself is not.
Quality? Usually yes, if you mean the physical magnitude inhering in a bearer or system. BFO defines quality exactly in that family.

There is another nuance: standards for quantities and units treat power as a Quantity Kind, and explicitly list power among observable properties that can be measured numerically. QUDT says a Quantity Kind includes things like length, mass, time, force, energy, and power, while a Quantity is the measurement of such a property for a particular object or system.

 That aligns very nicely with a BFO-style interpretation:
Power as a universal/type of measurable property → subclass of bfo:Quality
a particular actual power of a specific generator → instance of that quality class
the numeric record “4.5 MW” → information artifact/measurement datum, not the quality itself. 
	
Suggested child classes
power:GeneratedPower [001 - 002]
power:ConsumedPower  [001 - 003]

Formal Definition (DL)
power:Power ⊑BFO:Quality
power:GeneratedPower ⊑ power:Power
power:ConsumedPower⊑power:Power

Elucidation
Power represents electrical power as a domain quantity/quality relevant to offshore generation, transmission, and consumption contexts. The primary operational distinction required at this stage is between generated and consumed power.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO - power: Power  [001-001]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#PowerDemand -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#PowerDemand">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/core/Core/InformationContentEntity"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Suggested Reuse
Close to demand/profile modeling in SAREF4ENER.

Rationale: PowerDemand is directly related to:
a specific demand profile;
The BUS demand period datatype property.
That means PowerDemand is best treated as an information artifact specifying required electrical demand, not as a material entity or process.

Formal Definition (DL)

	power:PowerDemand ⊑BFO:GenericallyDependentContinuant
power:PowerDemand ⊑∃ power:hasPowerDemandProfile.power:PowerDemandProfile
power:PowerDemand ⊑∃ power:dependsOnDemandPeriod.power:BUS

Elucidation
A PowerDemand is a specified electrical-demand requirement associated with a BUS and constrained by the BUS demand period. It is interpreted through a demand profile that provides the defining curve or formula.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO - power:PowerDemand [002-001]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#PowerDemandProfile -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#PowerDemandProfile">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/core/Core/InformationContentEntity"/>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO - power:PowerDemandProfile [003-001]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#PowerGenerationModule -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#PowerGenerationModule">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/core/Core/EngineeredSystem"/>
        <rdfs:comment>BFO Classification
BFO:Object (subclass of BFO:MaterialEntity)
IOF Classification
Best aligned with iof-constr:EngineeredSystem (material artifact pattern)

Rationale
A PowerGenerationModule is a physical piece of equipment (e.g., turbine, generator unit) that participates in the offshore electrical architecture. It is not itself a role, but rather a material entity that can bear realizable entities, such as a power generation function or generation role, which are realized when the equipment is connected to the BUS and operating.

Formal Definition (DL)
power:PowerGenerationModule ⊑ BFO:Object
power:PowerGenerationModule ⊑ ∃ power:connectedToBUS.power:BUS
power:PowerGenerationModule ⊑ ∃ BFO:bearer_of.power:PowerGenerationFunction

Elucidation
A PowerGenerationModule is a material device designed to generate electrical power within a BUS-centered offshore system. Typical examples include gas turbines, diesel generators, or renewable generation units.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO - power:PowerGenerationModule [008-001]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#PowerTransmissionModule -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#PowerTransmissionModule">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://spec.industrialontologies.org/ontology/core/Core/Assembly"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Suggested Reuse
Conceptually related to SEAS electrical-connection semantics, but should remain your local engineered-equipment class.

Rationale
It is a physical module composed of transmission equipment and explicitly has booleans transmits and consumes. Therefore it is a material artifact/module.

Formal Definition (DL)
power:PowerTransmissionModule⊑BFO:Object
power:PowerTransmissionModule⊑∃power:transmits.xsd:boolean
power:PowerTransmissionModule⊑∃power:consumes.xsd:boolean

Elucidation
A PowerTransmissionModule is the physical equipment module responsible for transmitting electrical power in the offshore electrical architecture.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO -  power:PowerTransmissionModule [006-001]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#ProviderRole -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.usp.ai/Ontologies/FPEO/PowerModule.rdf#ProviderRole">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000023"/>
        <rdfs:comment>BFO Classification
BFO Type: BFO:Role
The class power:ProviderRole represents a specialization, characterizing the role assumed by an instance of power:FPSO when it acts as an energy provider in the system, being a context-dependent entity that describes the participation of the FPSO as an energy source in a given operational scenario, being able to coexist with other energy roles simultaneously and being determined by the operational conditions of the system, not constituting an intrinsic property of the material entity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>FPEO - power:ProviderRole [010-001]</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


